Translation patch: `misc::split_version_inner` (src/misc/mod.rs): index loops and the trusted `btoi`

Change: `input.split(|c| *c == b'.')` with `take(2).enumerate()` / `next()` / `?`, and `iter().position(..)`, become two helper loops `dot_or_end(input, start)` (index of the first `.` at or after `start`, else `input.len()`) and `digits_end(input)` (index of the first byte outside `b'0'..=b'9'`, else `input.len()`): the first and second parts are `input[..end0]` and `input[end0 + 1..end1]`, returning `None` if `input` has no first or no second `.`, and the third is `input[end1 + 1..end2]` truncated at `digits_end`. `btoi::btoi(chunk).ok()?` becomes a `match` on `crate::value::convert::trusted::btoi_u8(chunk)` (patch 40's helper, `btoi::btoi::<u8>(chunk).ok()`).
Why: Aeneas does not model `split`, `take`, `enumerate`, `position` or closures, nor `?` on `Option`; `btoi` is a dependency (patch 40 already routes it, with an exact Lean model).
Behaviour: Identical: the result is `Some` exactly when the input has at least two `.` and the first two parts and the digit prefix of the third part (up to the next `.` or the end) each parse as a `u8`, with those values; every other case is `None` in both, and `btoi` is pure, so the order of the checks does not matter.

